<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Starts Firebase Analytics with collection OFF, so that nothing is measured before the
    consent gateway has answered.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
    Firebase enables collection the moment its native SDK initialises, which is earlier than
    any managed code in this project can run - earlier than Boot, and long before UMP has been
    asked anything. So `AnalyticsSetup` calling SetAnalyticsCollectionEnabled(false) at start-up
    cannot close the window; it can only close it after the fact, which for a regulator is not
    closing it. The only thing that starts the SDK disabled is this flag, read out of the
    manifest before any of our code exists.

    WHAT IT COSTS
    Collection now depends entirely on AnalyticsSetup.Push() running - if that path ever breaks,
    the app measures nothing at all rather than measuring too much. That is the right way round
    for a consent gate and the wrong way round for a retention test, so the enable path is
    verified on a device rather than assumed: look for "[Analytics] collection enabled" in
    logcat on any launch where consent is granted.

    WHY AN ANDROIDLIB OF OUR OWN
    Not FirebaseApp.androidlib beside it, which the Firebase editor tooling generates from
    google-services.json and may rewrite. Not Assets/Plugins/Android/AndroidManifest.xml, which
    replaces Unity's launcher manifest rather than merging into it. A library manifest is merged,
    is ours, and is the smallest thing that can carry one flag.

    CONSENT MODE (invariant 55b)
    The four google_analytics_default_allow_* flags below start every consent mode purpose
    DENIED, which is Google's documented default for an app that asks later. They stay: an EEA
    player's first_open must not go out granted before UMP answers. Collection is switched on
    only after the splash's UMP answer has been applied with setConsent (MeasurementGate):
    Firebase logs first_open and session_start the moment collection turns on, and 1.1.4
    turned it on with these defaults still standing, seconds before the grant, so both went
    out as cookieless pings and GA reported no first opens. An owed consent form, or a UMP
    silent for three seconds, opens collection denied; with analytics_storage denied,
    Analytics sends events without any device or user identifier and models from them, and
    the real answer is applied when it comes. setConsent persists across launches, so these
    defaults only ever speak for a first launch.

    CRASHLYTICS
    firebase_crashlytics_collection_enabled starts crash reporting OFF for the same reason and
    on the same consent: AnalyticsSetup switches it on once the applied answer grants
    analytics_storage on an install that is not child-directed, and off again if that is
    withdrawn. The switch persists across launches, like setConsent.

    The package is a namespace rather than code: AGP 9 refuses a namespace shared by two modules
    (see Tools/make_appsflyer_connector.py for what that costs), so it must be unique.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
          package="com.tekoworld.glimmergroove.measurement"
          android:versionCode="1"
          android:versionName="1.0">

    <application>
        <meta-data
            android:name="firebase_analytics_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="firebase_crashlytics_collection_enabled"
            android:value="false" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="google_analytics_default_allow_analytics_storage"
            android:value="false" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="google_analytics_default_allow_ad_storage"
            android:value="false" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="google_analytics_default_allow_ad_user_data"
            android:value="false" />
        <meta-data
            android:name="google_analytics_default_allow_ad_personalization_signals"
            android:value="false" />
    </application>
</manifest>
